I keep having the same conversation.

Someone is trying to use AI in their writing workflow and it's not quite working. They ask: *Should I just get better at prompting? Should I write a style guide? Should I build an agent? Should I document my preferences somewhere? Should I upload my existing content?* And the honest answer is always some version of "yes, but in a specific order, and most of the value comes from the combination."

That conversation gets frustrating to have repeatedly without something to point at. So I wrote it down.

This is **CADI** — Collect, Analyze, Deploy, Iterate — a prescriptive path through the confusion. It's designed to be followed roughly in order, at least the first time through.

The name is deliberate. A caddie in golf walks the course with you. They carry your bag, read the conditions, advise the shot — but they don't take the swing. (For non-golfers: a caddie is the expert assistant who's paid to know more than you do about the conditions, but the swing is still yours.) That's the relationship this framework is trying to build with AI. It handles the load; you make the calls.

An experienced editor with access to your content can produce a first usable version of the guidance in a few hours by feeding curated examples to a language model and shaping what comes back. Then it's an editorial process: share it with colleagues, use it for a few days, fold their reactions in. Value compounds because each round sharpens the guidance, not because the work gets harder. It works for any content type or language, as long as you have enough of it to learn from.

---

## The problem: every prompt starts from zero

Most AI writing assistance starts cold every time. No memory of what worked last time. No sense of your voice, your standards, your audience. So it gives you something generic and confident, and you spend the rest of your time correcting it.

The fix isn't a better prompt. It's giving the AI a documented picture of what good looks like *for you*, and building a way to improve that picture as you learn what works. Done right, the AI gets more useful over time rather than requiring the same effort on every task.

There's a failure mode that persists even after you think you've solved this: **confident wrongness + silent drift**. The AI sounds authoritative but cites a source incorrectly. It hits approximately the right word count but adds a paragraph that subtly changes your meaning. These are harder to catch than obvious nonsense, and they accumulate faster.

---

## Where you probably are: supervised co-writing

Most people using AI for writing aren't doing "autonomous AI publishing." They're doing **supervised co-writing**: AI handles a first pass or a tedious task, a human reviews and corrects, a human publishes. That's normal; it's also where most of the real value is right now.

CADI is for teams who want to move from "AI is something we sometimes use" to "AI is something our editorial standards reliably shape." That transition doesn't happen through better prompting alone.

---

## Before you start: five things to check

This framework works best when you can answer yes to these:

- You have **50+ published items** you consider high-quality (not just recent)
- You have some signal for quality: engagement data, editor picks, longevity tags, or even manual curation
- At least one person has **time to own the guidance doc** and keep it current
- You have some way to **export content** from your CMS in text form (Markdown, HTML, CSV)
- Editors are willing to **log failures** rather than just silently fix them

You don't need a data science team, a custom CMS, or a formal AI strategy. But if no one will own the guidance document, the method breaks down at deployment.

---

## The CADI framework: Collect → Analyze → Deploy → Iterate

CADI is a four-phase loop. You build it once, then run the last phase continuously. Each cycle makes the guidance sharper, and the AI more useful.

The piece that makes it work, and that's missing from most AI-writing advice, is treating the guidance document itself as something you test and revise, not a one-time setup. C/A/D get you to a first version. I is where the value comes from.

I've arrived at this through practice, and written about the pieces separately. This is the first time I've named it.

### C — Collect: Extract your best examples

Your best content already embodies your patterns: word counts, title structures, tag usage, narrative flow. Don't grab the latest 50 published items; **curate the exemplary 50.**

**Start with one content subtype, not a mix.** Most organisations publish several things that look similar but follow different patterns: press releases, news briefs, feature reports, donor updates, blog posts. Each has its own voice and structure. Mixing them gives you average patterns that fit none of them. Pick the subtype that matters most to you right now and run CADI on that. Repeat for other subtypes later.

**Don't default to top-traffic items.** That's the obvious choice and the most common mistake. High traffic often correlates with topic timeliness, social-share luck, or audience reach, not with the qualities you'd want to reproduce. Three signals to combine instead:

- **Editorial investment** — items your team spent the most time on. Effort is a quality signal even when the numbers aren't there.
- **Aspirational fit** — items you'd want more of your output to look like.
- **Performance *for type*** — a press release that did 5× the median for press releases tells you more than a feature that hit Hacker News.

Document the criteria you used. It will matter when you audit later.

**Getting the files into the model.** Once you have your 50–100 items, the most practical path:

1. Export each item as HTML from your CMS (WordPress, Drupal, and most custom systems support this).
2. Feed the HTML to an agent with a preprocessing prompt that strips chrome (navigation, ads, related-content modules) and converts each file into a standardized Markdown structure with a metadata header. The [starter kit](/resources/cadi-starter-kit.txt) includes the preprocessing prompt.
3. Concatenate the cleaned Markdown into a single file with clear delimiters between items.
4. Feed that consolidated document to the analyzing model. [NotebookLM](https://notebooklm.google.com/) handles this step particularly well; Claude Projects and ChatGPT also work.

This is one-time setup per content subtype.

**Ready to move on when**: you have 50–100 items in a single consolidated, cleaned document; you've written down why they qualify; and an editor has spot-checked for obvious outliers.

For implementation detail, including Drupal export code and a worked UNDRR case study, see [Improving AI chatbots with an editorial handbook from your best content](/posts/evidence-based-content-guidelines-drupal-ai/).

### A — Analyze: Find quantitative patterns

Feed your curated examples to an LLM with a structured prompt to extract:
- Average word counts (with ranges, by content type)
- Title formats (% using colons, question marks, etc.)
- Heading usage frequency
- Metadata usage (tag counts, co-occurrence patterns)
- Tone markers (passive vs. active, hedging language)

AI is exceptionally good at this extraction step; humans do the interpretation. The output is not guidance yet; it's **empirical description** of what your best content actually looks like.

**Ready to move on when**: you have a written summary of patterns with numbers, and at least one editor has reviewed it and flagged anything that looks wrong or unrepresentative.

### D — Deploy: Turn patterns into working instructions

Combine what the analysis found with your existing institutional knowledge (style guides, brand standards, domain constraints) into a single document your AI tool can use as its standing instructions.

This step requires editorial judgment: observed patterns and existing style rules will sometimes conflict. If the analysis says your best titles average 8 words but house style caps them at 6, decide which wins and log why. Write it down; that decision belongs in your change log, not left implicit.

Result: instructions specific enough for the AI to act on ("180 words ± 10%") instead of vague ("be concise"). The same document works for human editors too.

**Ready to move on when**: the instructions have been reviewed by at least one editor who wasn't involved in writing them, and they've been uploaded or integrated with your AI tool.

### I — Iterate: Build confidence over time

Iteration done badly is just tweaking in the dark, which is how we started. Done well, it's hypothesis-driven:

- "IF we add explicit numeric targets ('180 words ± 10%'), THEN first-pass acceptance rises from 40% to 70%"
- "IF we show exemplars of title styles, THEN AI chooses appropriate titles 85% of the time"
- "IF we run oppositional review (a second model critiques our guidance), THEN we catch biased rules 80% of the time"

Then test them: run a simple before/after comparison on real content, measure outcomes with a concrete metric, and document what changed and why.

For a small team (2–3 editors), you need at least 20–30 items per condition to see a reliable signal; fewer than that, the result could just be noise. Blinding reviewers doesn't need to be complicated: just split the batch and don't tell the reviewer which version of the instructions produced which output.

**Mid-cycle revision trigger**: if more than 20% of outputs in a week fail the same check, don't wait for the quarterly audit. That's a pattern worth investigating now.

This approach isn't invented from scratch. It's borrowed from disciplines that have been doing this for decades:

- **Medical guideline development**: AGREE II and GRADE for assessing whether guidelines are evidence-based and implementable
- **Design systems**: Diátaxis for organizing knowledge by user need, not topic
- **Journalism**: content analysis to audit whether outlets meet their own stated standards

The shift is applying those disciplines to editorial AI workflows.

None of that is new. What I haven't seen elsewhere is the specific combination: deriving guidance from your own best content corpus, then treating that guidance as a testable hypothesis rather than a fixed rule. Prior art welcome.

**Ready to move on when**: you have at least one completed hypothesis test, a documented decision (kept or rejected), and a failure log that someone is actually adding to.

